[32m+[m[32m/**[m
[32m+[m[32m * WHY: the first material a card got was one gradient over the whole plate, and on[m
[32m+[m[32m * a real terminal it measured twelve of 255 above the page at the top row and four[m
[32m+[m[32m * at the foot — over a page of 28. Nobody could see it. Worse, the gradient's lower[m
[32m+[m[32m * half ran BELOW the page, because "fall" mixed the bottom row toward black, so the[m
[32m+[m[32m * bottom of every card faded into the page it was supposed to be standing on.[m
[32m+[m[32m *[m
[32m+[m[32m * The class this closes is "a surface that is not a surface": any elevation a card[m
[32m+[m[32m * claims that the eye cannot find, in either direction, on either kind of terminal.[m
[32m+[m[32m * Four ways to land in it, and each has a case here:[m
[32m+[m[32m *[m
[32m+[m[32m *   1. NO LADDER. One wash gives the eye no edge. A card is a header tray, a body[m
[32m+[m[32m *      plate and a recessed footer tray, and the assertions read that ordering out[m
[32m+[m[32m *      of the painted bytes rather than trusting the constants.[m
[32m+[m[32m *   2. A ZONE THAT SINKS INTO THE PAGE. Every zone must stand strictly off the[m
[32m+[m[32m *      ground, at the top row and at the last body row alike.[m
[32m+[m[32m *   3. THE WRONG DIRECTION. On a light terminal, a lift toward white is invisible.[m
[32m+[m[32m *      Both grounds are swept, and a ground added to the sweep must be decided for.[m
[32m+[m[32m *   4. AN INSET THAT IS NOT AN INSET. A two-pane card sets its side column into the[m
[32m+[m[32m *      body, which only reads as a split if the two materials differ AND the inset[m
[32m+[m[32m *      stops at the hairline. The subject there is the shipped `/settings` card.[m
[32m+[m[32m *[m
[32m+[m[32m * The subject is always the production seam — `renderModalShell` then[m
[32m+[m[32m * `applyModalReveal`, and the real `SettingsSelectorComponent` for the inset —[m
[32m+[m[32m * because a constant asserted against itself proves nothing about the card.[m
